`formatCheckDigit` is squarely in category 2 by nature — a ~15-line, well-known standalone algorithm (reverse scan, doubling, digit-sum, mod 10) that is a pure function of a single `String` (`customerCode`) — yet it's been dropped into category 1 as the only method on the interface carrying real algorithmic loop/branch logic inline. That's the structural inconsistency a maintainer here would flag.

**Suggested change:**
- Add a small util (e.g. `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.util.LuhnCheckDigit` with a static `compute(String)` / `checkDigit(String)`), mirroring `LocalityResolver`/`TelephoneNormalizer`.
- Reduce `formatCheckDigit` to the same thin shape as `formatLocality`:
  ```java
  @Named("formatCheckDigit")
  default Integer formatCheckDigit(Owner owner) {
      if (owner == null || owner.getCustomerCode() == null) {
          return null;
      }
      return LuhnCheckDigit.compute(owner.getCustomerCode());
  }
  ```

This keeps the mapper focused on mapping/field-shaping and puts the reusable algorithm where the codebase already puts reusable algorithms, instead of letting the interface accrete general-purpose computation. The OpenAPI schema addition itself is fine and consistent.
